+Curve stripping chooses the points automatically, and it is what happens when
+nothing is specified.  Two optional columns given to `PKNCAconc()` change that:
+`exclude_half.life` drops specific points and then curve strips whatever
+remains, while `include_half.life` names the exact points to fit and switches
+curve stripping off entirely.  The decision tree below shows how to choose
+between them and what each one changes.
+
+## Choosing a Point Selection Method
+
+```{r decision-tree-options, echo=FALSE}
+# Named in both the figure and the text below.  Fail the build if one is
+# renamed or removed rather than describing options that no longer exist.
+hl_auto_options <-
+  c("min.hl.points", "allow.tmax.in.half.life", "adj.r.squared.factor")
+stopifnot(all(hl_auto_options %in% names(PKNCA.options())))
+hl_auto_options_md <-
+  paste0(
+    paste0("`", utils::head(hl_auto_options, -1), "`", collapse = ", "),
+    ", and `", utils::tail(hl_auto_options, 1), "`"
+  )
+```
+
+```{r decision-tree, echo=FALSE, fig.width=8.4, fig.height=5.2, dpi=192, out.width="100%", fig.alt="Decision tree for choosing between automatic curve stripping, exclude_half.life, and include_half.life.  Automatic curve stripping is the default and needs neither column.  exclude_half.life drops the points flagged TRUE and curve strips the rest.  include_half.life keeps only the points flagged TRUE and fits them directly.  In every case, BLQ and zero concentrations and points at or before the end of the last dose are dropped before fitting."}
+# The tree is drawn in the coordinate system of the figure itself (840 wide by
+# 520 tall, y increasing downward) so that positions read as pixels.
+tree_box <- function(x, y, w, h, fill = "white") {
+  rect(x, y, x + w, y + h, border = "black", lwd = 1.5, col = fill)
+}
+tree_arrow <- function(x, y0, y1) {
+  arrows(x, y0, x, y1, length = 0.08, angle = 20, lwd = 1.5)
+}
+
+cex_body <- 0.78
+cex_head <- 0.9
+branch_x <- c(140, 420, 700)
+branch_x_left <- branch_x - 125
+
+op <- par(mar = rep(0, 4))
